<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wang Shichong</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 621</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wang Shichong was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> general who held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> during the dynasty's collapse. In 619 he deposed the Sui emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yang-tong">Yang Tong</a> and proclaimed his own state of Zheng. Besieged by the Tang under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-tang">Li Shimin</a>, he lost his ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dou-jiande">Dou Jiande</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hulao">Battle of Hulao</a> in 621 and surrendered; he was murdered on his way into exile.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wang-shichong.jpg" alt="Wang Shichong" width="320" height="459" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E7%8E%8B%E4%B8%96%E5%85%85%EF%BC%88%E8%AA%AA%E5%94%90%E6%BC%94%E7%BE%A9%E5%85%A8%E5%82%B3%EF%BC%89.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Wang Shichong was born in 567. His family descended from the Zhi clan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-regions">Western Regions</a>, presumably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a>, and took the name Wang when his widowed grandmother remarried. Studious in military strategy and the law, he rose through the army and by 610 supervised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yang-of-sui">Emperor Yang</a>'s palace at Jiangdu. In 613 he defeated and killed the rebel Liu Yuanjin south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>, then slaughtered his men after promising to spare them if they surrendered. In 615 he marched north to relieve the emperor, besieged by the Turks at Yanmen, and was made acting governor of Jiangdu in 616.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 617 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yang-of-sui">Emperor Yang</a> sent him to defend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> against the rebel Li Mi. After the emperor was killed in 618, Wang joined the officials who proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yang-tong">Yang Tong</a> emperor and became one of the seven nobles who ruled in his name. When his colleagues made peace with Li Mi, Wang struck first: he killed Lu Chu, had Yuan Wendu handed over and executed, and took all power. In the autumn of 618 he defeated Li Mi at the Battle of Yanshi, and most of Li's territory submitted to Luoyang.</p><p>After taking the throne Wang created his son Wang Xuanying crown prince and nineteen relatives princes. When a plot to restore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yang-tong">Yang Tong</a> was uncovered, Wang Shichong had the former emperor forced to drink poison and, when that failed, strangled. A Tang army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-tang">Li Shimin</a>, son of Emperor Gaozu, attacked in the autumn of 620 and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> in the spring of 621. Wang's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catapult">catapults</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crossbow">crossbows</a> inflicted heavy losses, but after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dou-jiande">Dou Jiande</a> was captured at Hulao, Wang left the city and surrendered.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, Wang told Emperor Gaozu that the Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> had promised to spare him. Gaozu reduced him to a commoner and ordered him and his clan into exile in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dou-jiande">Dou Jiande</a> was executed. Dugu Xiude, whose father Dugu Ji had been executed by Wang, then killed him and his brother Wang Shiyun to avenge his father, and was punished only with the loss of his post. The rest of the clan were executed after plotting rebellion on the way into exile. Traditional historians reviled Wang; Liu Xu, editor of the Old Book of Tang, called him a wicked man who happened to encounter an incompetent ruler.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wang Shichong” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Tang prince Li Shimin, besieging <b>Wang Shichong</b> in Luoyang, held the Hulao Pass against Dou Jiande's relieving Xia army, routed it and captured Dou.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-28">28 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Blockaded in Luoyang for months, <b>Wang Shichong</b> led his troops out and surprised Li Mi's besieging army north of Yanshi, winning a decisive victory.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-6">6 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 621 he marched to relieve <b>Wang Shichong</b>, besieged at Luoyang by the Tang.</blockquote><a href="/state/xia-dou-jiande">Xia (Dou Jiande)</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Wang Shichong</b>, a general of Central Asian descent, defended Luoyang for Emperor Yang against the rebel Li Mi.</blockquote><a href="/state/zheng-wang-shichong">Zheng (Wang Shichong)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35,108&amp;year=611" aria-label="Asia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Wang Shichong"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4165 4529h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4165 4529h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35,108&amp;year=611">What was here in 611 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
